81	- 2026-09-25, row 39's condition resolved: the override probe passed at the first attempt in both vocabularies, so the diagonal's product is the override of `mul` on `Diag extends Matrix`, landed in `run-c4/src/FlatArrays.{fss,fsi}` and `apl/mg/FlatArrays2.{fss,fsi}`; the old operator was not built; row 39 is closed (ledger row 299; `run-c4/probes/vocabulary/diag-override.md`).
82	- 2026-09-25, on the wording of protocol § 6's three rules after an Opus tone review of the coordinator's reply that proposed them (slogans, absolutes, passive voice on the failure, a shape offered with no alternatives): "Leave protocol as is" — the plain wording committed in `38829a636` stands. And the session's model is switched to Opus after this compaction at his request, "to get a feel for it"; he wants to consider pinning the workflow's escalated judge, which today inherits the session's model, explicitly to Fable, a note for the coordinator after the compaction to put to him.
83	- 2026-09-25, after the diagonal probe's timings came out about twice the 09-15 captures for no known reason: "we should always instruct workers when they record timings to record also the CPU so that we can guesstimate the comparisons" — protocol § 6.
84	- 2026-09-25, row 40 of the re-approval table (the three questions of `coordinator/array-design.md` left at the worker's defaults on 2026-09-19: a store per element type chosen by the factory under the library's generic objects, the size also carried as a field on the store, a factory for a size known only at run time returning the unsized `Array`), after it was re-explained with the library's own way first, what changed since and the peers: "close as no decision taken". None of the three stands as a default; nothing is reverted, since nothing was built; the three questions return with the array design after the switch-over, through protocol § 6's method (a clean worker's list of every way the language and the library offer, the library's own way first, the nine steps), with the review's findings (`reviews/array-design-review.md`: the first refuted by probe, the second right and forced, the third stopping microGPT at the checker) and the ground moved since (one library; design B, whose factory can make a size's descriptor from a number known only at run time, `perf-probes/nat/size-rung-probes.md`) as their input. The same hour he asked why microGPT uses `RR64` and not `RR32`, his premise being that LLMs are trained at FP32 at most; the reason on record is the Python reference's float64 goldens (`experiment/run-c-goldens/README.md`); adding the element width to the returning array questions was recommended and is not yet answered.
85	- 2026-09-26, row 47 of the re-approval table (climb batch 2's gate judge changing the `testSystem` comparison from shard by shard to the sum of the four shards, and deferring a comparison of test names): "Agreed with recommendation" — the sum stays for batch 4, as batches 3 and 3.5 ran; the name comparison (red when a test that passed before is missing or failing) comes later as a script change put to him on its own. Row 48 (the last landed summary as the comparand) was not taken; batch 4 runs under it as it stands. In the same message: "Let's run batch 4 overnight", taken as the go for a manifest held to the items he decided on 2026-09-24 (the interpreter coercion rung, the specification rung with its decision record, the size wall's checker rung with `keep-size-params.patch`, rows 379 and 380/381 as rungs if they share no file), any rung needing a decision not on record held out for him.
86	- 2026-09-26, the judge-tier ask left for the coordinator after the session moved to Opus: "Second ruling is for Fable." — a judge's second ruling on the same rung or tree runs on Fable, named in `coordinator/climb-batch-workflow.js` (`judgeTier`), no longer the session's model; first rulings stay on Opus; Fable is spent only when a second ruling happens. `coordinator/repair-batch-workflow.js` is unchanged (not used for batch 4).
87	- 2026-09-26, on who commits a worker's output: "Workers should be committing themselves as they go, focused their files only. Protects against container failures and stops from harness bothering you about uncommitted files, that you are hiding under `.` replies." — replacing the coordinator's practice of briefing workers to commit nothing and committing their files after review; written into protocol § 5 with the push check the batch's gather needs, and sent to the two workers then running (S1, S2).
88	- 2026-09-26, climb batch 4's held push, rung C's stop (any interpreter test's output changing) met by three tests whose outputs differ only in Java line numbers (`XXXimmutableTopLevel`, through the two files C edited) and object identity hashes (`taskTrace2`, `taskTrace3`), identical once those are masked (`compile-ladder/rung-interp-coercion/JUDGE.md`, "For Pavol" item 1): "(a), push" — read as outside the stop's purpose (nothing converts in them); C, N and K land as gathered, and S1's note (`reviews/spec-change-form.md`, committed by its worker, whose push the permission system refused) is pushed with them. O stays stopped for his decision.
89	- 2026-09-26, rung O of climb batch 4 (row 379), after the clean list of `reviews/wrap-dependent-code.md`: "Note from O looks solid to me. Don't see any credible alternative, so unless you do let's put that on the record." — the note's option 1, the specification's own way: the wrapping operators ∔ `DOTPLUS`, ∸ `DOTMINUS` (unary and binary) and ⨰ `DOTTIMES` (`Specification/basic/operators/opr-overview.tex:172-176`, `:205-209`) are declared on `Integral[\I\]` and on `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `NN32`, `NN64` and `ZZ` in the interpreter's library, bound to wrapping natives of their own beside the checked ones; the library bodies that mean to wrap (the six `split-1` sites and the `+1` sites of `Library/RangeInternals.fss`, `Random.fss`'s `LinearCongruential`, `ChunkedSparseArray.fss:76`, `IntMap.fss:676`) and the five team tests that assert or rely on wrapping (`intPrim`, `longPrim`, `HeapTest`, `QuickCheckTest`, `ReflectiveQuickCheckTest`) are rewritten with them, every expression fully parenthesised since the operators' precedence is stated only among themselves, and the strided distance that must not overflow is reordered and keeps the checked operators; then the ten natives raise `IntegerOverflow` (rung O's `natives.patch`), with the count of changed interpreter tests measured again and expected at zero. The saturating family waits for a step of its own. This decides row 348 for the specification's spelling: at the switch-over the compiler prelude's reversed spellings, in 10 compiled test files, are respelled. The coordinator saw no credible alternative (the note's option 2 restructures every body with no operator and leaves the specification's spelling undeclared; option 3 adds the saturating family now, beyond the need). Open, for the rung's manifest: `NN32` and `NN64` wrap under walk as well (bare `iadd`/`lmul` natives), outside row 379's ten natives.
90	- 2026-09-26, S1 of climb batch 4's specification rung, how the revised specification shows a change (`reviews/spec-change-form.md`): "S1 — 1, teams' way." — the layered form the team used for 1.0 and in its 2009 draft: a labelled callout at each changed passage that prints in every build (not a `\note`, which would read as the team's and vanish in a release build); a revival section in the changes appendix (Appendix I, `Specification/appendices/changes.tex`), each entry with the reason, the original sentences quoted with their path and line in the unrevised copy, and route C with what reversing to it would take; one paragraph in the front matter; the full reasoning in a repository document. What the unrevised copy is called in those citations waits on the specification-lineage note (`coordinator/spec-lineage.md`, in flight), since `Specification-1.0-frozen/` holds the 2011 draft, not 1.0.
91	- 2026-09-26, on the lineage note (`coordinator/spec-lineage.md` with the coordinator's check): asked whether the Types chapter of the team's unfinished restart (`Documentation/Specification/Prose/Language/types.tick`, Luchangco, 2012-05-31) counts as the designers' later word wherever it covers something, he quoted the recommendation, "on the rule S revises, it is the designers' latest intent, and the goal is to finish what they intended", and answered "Agreed." So `Specification/` stays the standard, and the Types chapter is cited beside it wherever it covers a topic: the rule as "instantiation exclusion" in its two-part form, the `covariant` modifier, `Bottom` inexpressible. One sentence saying so was added to the goal in CLAUDE.md. The S2 ask (the judgement's per-example verdicts for rung S) is still open.
92	- 2026-09-26, S2 of the specification rung (`reviews/spec-refused-examples-judgement.md`, the Fable judgement): "S2 yes, take the verdicts for rung S". Rung S, now in batch 5, revises each refused example to the one library's shape:
93	  - the where-clause section's empty list becomes `object Empty[\T\] extends List[\T\]`;
94	  - the covariance example becomes the library's widening function with bounded static parameters;
95	  - "a subtrait of every instantiation" stays as the counterexample that introduces the rule;
96	  - `convenience.tex`'s `Nothing` becomes `Nothing[\T\]`, with no `excludes`, and the exception chapters' defaults become `Nothing[\String\]` and `Nothing[\Exception\]`;
97	  - `Object` (and `Tuple`, whose prose leans on it) loses the algebraic supertraits, with `===` an operator on `Any`;
98	  - the internal appendix gets one sentence at each of its three passages;
99	  - each original is kept in the text as "Not allowed", the specification's own form (`basic/traits.tex:286-292`);
100	  - the rule is stated once, in Luchangco's two-part form under "instantiation exclusion": which instantiations exclude each other in `basic/types-vals-vars.tex`, the rule on declarations in `basic/traits.tex`;
101	  - the 2012 `covariant` keyword goes into the decision record.
102	  Asked the same hour whether the keyword is future work or on the critical path, he was told it is future work: no library declaration and no microGPT line uses it. It comes as two ledger entries among the S2 items: a defect row (the checker accepts it, the compiled run crashes, walk refuses) and worklist item 12 reworded to declaration-site variance by the keywords. Still open from the judgement's section 11, one per message:
103	  - the tower chapters' sequencing with the flattening rung;
104	  - whether sizes and boolean arguments count in the rule;
105	  - row 331 re-gated on static-argument inference;
106	  - worklist item 12;
107	  - `RationalQuantity`;
108	  - whether the keyword is mentioned in the text (default: record only);
109	  - the five candidate ledger rows;
110	  - what S1's citations call the unrevised copy.
111	  `CLIMB-BATCH-4.md`'s option (b) is corrected when S is briefed.
112	- 2026-09-26, the two ledger entries for the 2012 `covariant` keyword (the judgement's candidate row 1 and its § 8 proposal for worklist item 12), put to him as future work, not on the path to microGPT: "Agreed." Row 404 is appended, a soundness hole: the checker accepts variance and neither path carries it to run time. Its expected-failure tests are owed in a batch rung. Worklist item 12 is reworded to declaration-site variance by the keywords, carried to run time on both paths. In the same message he confirmed S2 ("Yes. Sure."). The judgement's candidate rows 2-5 are still open; its row 4 (sizes in the rule) is already row 402, opened by rung N.
113	- 2026-09-26, the number chapters under S2 (the judgement's § 3.3): "Agreed" to option 1. The tower chapters (`basic-lib/basic-integers.tex`, `basic-lib/numbers.tex`, `advanced-lib/numbers-advanced.tex`) change in the same batch as the flattening rung, never earlier. Rung S in a batch without the flattening rung leaves them untouched and lists them as deferred, with the reason. When the two share a batch, the gather checks the specification's declarations against the landed library. This narrows his 09-24 assignment for those chapters only. In the same message he asked whether everything held for the next batch should be split so that each step stays in sync, and asked for the project's state and an ordered plan of every open issue toward the running compiler: "I need you to help me with the high level judgment and plan coordination."
114	- 2026-09-26, the plan (the coordinator's page https://claude.ai/artifact/3CKVcCdTRC5SJA2hz5Fk5C, built from `coordinator/open-items-2026-09-26.md`): "Yes, this order and workers are go." The order is six phases:
115	  1. batch 5: the wrapping operators, rung S without the number chapters, and the run-time size rung;

